WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.510
It's almost impossible to think of roles like
James Bond or Han Solo in the hands of different

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:10.600
actors, but these are just 2 roles that could
have been totally unlike their present form.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.390
Imagine how differently these classics would
be if casting went the way the filmmakers

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:15.460
originally planned.

00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:21.199
Jurassic Park is such a well-crafted movie
it's hard to imagine anything changing.

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:25.819
The effects hold up surprisingly well decades
later, the pacing is tight, Jeff Goldblum

00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:28.140
is still impossibly sexy.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.310
It's a great film.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:33.800
However, the lead role of Doctor Alan Grant,
who's played in the movie by Sam Neill, was

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.379
originally offered to Harrison Ford, for reasons
that are obvious if you think about it.

00:00:38.379 --> 00:00:43.879
Grant was a gruff, rowdy scientist, paleontologist
rather than archaeologist in his case, who

00:00:43.879 --> 00:00:47.840
could effectively manage jungle territory
while pursued by a deadly enemy.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.120
He even had a cool floppy hat.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.219
As effectively as Sam Neill brought the character
to life, we all know we'd throw money at a

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:57.360
film about Indiana Jones versus dinosaurs.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.929
Unfortunately, Ford saw it differently.

00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:04.350
He compared the idea to putting Indiana Jones
on Mars, an idea that evidently made more

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:10.220
sense to him a decade and a half later, when
they actually had Indiana Jones meeting spacemen.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.180
You jumped in a refrigerator to escape an
atomic bomb, Ford.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.400
You couldn't punch a dinosaur?

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.880
Speaking of Harrison Ford, he almost never
had a chance to perform either of his most

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.970
closely identified characters.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:26.070
The roles of Indiana Jones in Raiders of the
Lost Ark and Han Solo in the first Star Wars

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:31.040
film were originally offered to Tom Selleck,
who turned them both down.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.530
This actually made sense at the time, believe
it or not.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:38.740
The original draft of Star Wars: A New Hope
imagined Han Solo as much older than Luke

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.950
Skywalker, like a kind of "black sheep" uncle
figure, which honestly could have been confusing

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.830
with all the other themes of familial connection
in the film.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:52.100
By the time Raiders of the Lost Ark was being
filmed, Ford had been in three of George Lucas'

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.790
films, American Graffiti and the first two
Star Wars movies, and Lucas worried about

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.000
becoming known for re-using certain actors.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.910
He gave Selleck another shot, but it didn't
work out, as Selleck was already committed

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:08.140
to his series Magnum P.I., and the network
wouldn't let him take the film.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.730
Losing out on Raiders was a particularly bitter
pill for Selleck, who ended up cooling his

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:17.340
heels in Hawaii during an actors' strike while
Indiana Jones and company were also on the

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.340
island.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.820
Selleck explained with some regret:

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.980
"I go to Hawaii to start Magnum, the actors
go on strike, and I had given my deposit to

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.310
a landlady and I couldn't afford a security
deposit.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.940
So I start working as a handyman, in Hawaii,
with no job.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.280
And guess who comes to Hawaii to finish their
movie?

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.860
Raiders of the Lost Ark.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.520
So I could've done them both."

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.520
Matt Damon has turned down a lot of major
movie roles.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.650
One of the most surprising: he almost played
conflicted paraplegic former Marine Jake Sully

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:50.560
in James Cameron's sprawling sci-fi action
film Avatar.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.860
The decision was tough on Damon, not only
because he was a huge fan of Cameron's, but

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.900
also because he figured the star potential
of the film could encourage the actor who

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:05.400
took his place (in this case Sam Worthington)
to potentially take jobs from him in the future.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.510
Unfortunately scheduling conflicts got in
the way, leaving us to imagine what could

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.510
have been.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.020
"What was he thinking?"

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:18.480
"What was he possibly…I, I, I have no defense,
other than that there was an actual scheduling

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.800
conflict."

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.600
While most of the Kill Bill movies' action
follows the ultraviolent exploits and tribulations

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.790
of the vengeful Bride, the character Bill
looms large over both films.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.610
C'mon, his name's right there in the title.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:36.290
His particular cruelties, obsessions, and
motivations trickle through his assassin minions

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.780
and even the Bride herself.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:42.910
All of which is to explain why, when Warren
Beatty turned down the titular role, Quentin

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.910
Tarantino was forced to do a complete rewrite
to compensate.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:48.050
Tarantino explained:

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:53.069
"The Warren Beatty thing is interesting, because
once I cast David Carradine, I did a lot of

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:58.020
little rewrites... actually shifting that
character into David's sphere.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.810
And it was really interesting reading that
first draft, because that's the Warren Beatty

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:02.849
version.

00:04:02.849 --> 00:04:05.810
He's more of a James Bond type character."

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.720
It's honestly hard to imagine how that film
would have turned out.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.190
It certainly would have been a far cry from
the gritty suburban samurai film we wound

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.200
up seeing.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.439
Once the James Bond producers dumped Pierce
Brosnan, a few curious shoulders were tapped.

00:04:19.439 --> 00:04:23.039
Looking to shake things up, and impressed
with his sharp-dressed killer performance

00:04:23.039 --> 00:04:28.150
in American Psycho, producer Barbara Broccoli
had a meeting with Bale to offer him the role

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:29.620
as 007.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.409
However Bale, a British actor himself, was
loath to take on a role that embodied what

00:04:34.409 --> 00:04:38.250
he considered the worst British stereotypes
all bundled together.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:43.060
He was reportedly cordial in his refusal,
but at one point quipped, quote, "I've already

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.480
played a serial killer."

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.800
Clearly he's not a fan.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.240
Nicolas Cage has turned down almost as many
memorable roles as he's taken.

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:57.039
His...how should we put this nicely?...curiously
varied career...has led him all over the map,

00:04:57.039 --> 00:05:00.039
even when he wasn't searching for National
Treasure.

00:05:00.039 --> 00:05:04.270
Among the many interesting oddities on his
résumé: he's turned down two roles that

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:09.629
went to Keaunu Reeves, Neo in The Matrix and
the title character in Constantine.

00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:13.270
According to Cage, he turned down The Matrix
because he wasn't interested in flying to

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.840
Australia to film it.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.909
While he was initially more enthusiastic about
Constantine, he ended up departing the project

00:05:19.909 --> 00:05:24.629
after original director Tarsem Singh dropped
out, sparking a pair of lawsuits between Singh

00:05:24.629 --> 00:05:25.909
and the studio.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.640
By the time the dust settled, Reeves was on
board.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.240
Another actor considered for the role of Neo
in The Matrix was Will Smith, and although

00:05:33.249 --> 00:05:37.689
it ended up launching a blockbuster trilogy,
Smith seems really at peace with turning down

00:05:37.689 --> 00:05:39.949
a massive once-in-a-lifetime role.

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:44.029
Speaking to Wired, he admitted that a project
like The Matrix is really difficult to sell

00:05:44.029 --> 00:05:48.830
in a pitch, and that when the movie was first
described to him, quote, "he didn't see it."

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:53.050
After watching the film later, Smith also
felt Reeves was a better fit, saying,

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.279
"I would have absolutely messed up The Matrix.

00:05:56.279 --> 00:05:59.909
At that point I wasn't smart enough of an
actor to let the movie be.

00:05:59.909 --> 00:06:02.410
Whereas Keanu was smart enough to let it be.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:06.259
Just let the movie and the director tell the
story and don't try to perform every moment."

00:06:06.259 --> 00:06:11.490
However, he doesn't have quite that level
of zen-like peace about Wild Wild West, the

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.369
film he accepted instead of The Matrix.

00:06:14.369 --> 00:06:18.369
Although the movie was a financial success,
critics tore it to pieces and in hindsight

00:06:18.369 --> 00:06:23.130
he admits his performance was a cash grab,
and led him to a vow never to take on a project

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.509
again just for the money.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.640
He presumably made his vow while reclining
on a pile of cash.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.440
"Oh!

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:34.880
Oh!"

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:39.869
Al Pacino is possibly one of the greatest
actors that ever lived, with a career bringing

00:06:39.869 --> 00:06:43.770
to life some of the most memorable characters
in modern cinema.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:48.400
Which makes it even more incredible to consider
what might have been for any number of roles.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.749
Naturally, every director wanted a piece of
Pacino at his peak; unfortunately, he couldn't

00:06:52.749 --> 00:06:54.409
be everywhere at once.

00:06:54.409 --> 00:06:59.000
At various times, he had the potential to
be Han Solo in Star Wars, Willard in Apocalypse

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:05.849
Now, Axel Foley from Beverly Hills Cop, John
McClane from Die Hard, and even freaking Rambo.

00:07:05.849 --> 00:07:09.479
Instead of being on a couple posters in your
college dorm room, he could potentially have

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.400
been on all of them.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.719
Through the '90s and into the early 2000s,
the name "Bruce Willis" was synonymous with

00:07:15.719 --> 00:07:17.089
"action hero."

00:07:17.089 --> 00:07:21.849
That's almost entirely due to his role as
John McClane, a wisecracking regular cop thrust

00:07:21.849 --> 00:07:26.189
into extraordinary life or death situations
in a string of Die Hard films.

00:07:26.189 --> 00:07:30.979
When the original Die Hard hit theaters in
1988, Willis was seen as an unconventional,

00:07:30.979 --> 00:07:34.809
if not risky choice to headline a full-throttle
action movie.

00:07:34.809 --> 00:07:39.380
At the time, Willis was known almost entirely
for his Emmy-nominated role as cheeky private

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:43.159
detective David Addison on the TV rom-com
Moonlighting.

00:07:43.159 --> 00:07:48.419
According to screenwriter Steven E. de Souza
in Die Hard: An Oral History, filmmakers hired

00:07:48.419 --> 00:07:51.099
Willis, quote, "in desperation."

00:07:51.099 --> 00:07:55.339
Every big action hero and tough guy actor
of the '80s passed on Die Hard, including

00:07:55.339 --> 00:08:01.080
Arnold Schwarzenegger, Sylvester Stallone,
James Caan, Burt Reynolds, and Richard Gere.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.560
Deadpool marked a major comeback for Ryan
Reynolds, whose once-promising career had

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.970
been knocked off course by a few bombs, chief
among them the 2010 DC Comics movie Green

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:11.970
Lantern.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:15.869
The superhero adventure was both a critical
and commercial failure, earning back about

00:08:15.869 --> 00:08:19.839
half its $200 million budget at the American
box office.

00:08:19.839 --> 00:08:21.249
Reynolds explained to Variety,

00:08:21.249 --> 00:08:24.279
"After Green Lantern, I was pretty much unhireable."

00:08:24.279 --> 00:08:27.449
But while his star was falling, another guy's
was rising.

00:08:27.449 --> 00:08:33.500
Jon Hamm, the annually Emmy-nominated star
of the AMC 1960s period piece Mad Men, seems

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:39.560
to have dodged a big green bullet, as DC aggressively
pursued him to be a big-screen superhero.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:40.560
Hamm told GQ,

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.130
"They came after me pretty hard for Green
Lantern.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:46.400
But I was like, meh, that's not what I want
to do."

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:51.269
In the 1990s, Leonardo DiCaprio was the boy
wonder of Hollywood, finding fame, fortune,

00:08:51.269 --> 00:08:56.380
and teen idol status with roles like troubled
teen Luke on Growing Pains, Jack Dawson in

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:00.770
Titanic, and Romeo in Baz Luhrrmann's Romeo
and Juliet.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:05.490
It only makes sense that the fresh-faced box-office
draw would be given the chance to play actual

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:10.930
"boy wonder" Robin, a.k.a. Dick Grayson, in
Warner Brothers' ongoing Batman franchise.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:15.690
In 2015, he told ShortList that he was under
consideration for the part, which would've

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:19.259
started with 1995's Batman Forever, saying.

00:09:19.259 --> 00:09:21.130
"I never screen-tested.

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:23.279
I had a meeting with Joel Schumacher.

00:09:23.279 --> 00:09:26.339
It was just one meeting, and no, I didn't
end up doing it.

00:09:26.339 --> 00:09:29.880
As I recall I took the meeting, but didn't
want to play the role."

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:33.530
DiCaprio mentioned two other parts in big
movies that he met with filmmakers about,

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:38.579
before taking himself out of contention: Anakin
Skywalker in the Star Wars prequels, and Spider-Man

00:09:38.579 --> 00:09:40.760
in Spider-Man.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:46.560
In 1996 and 1997, Will Smith starred in the
back-to-back summer blockbusters Independence

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.600
Day and Men in Black.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.910
The movies marked his official transition
from sitcom star to Hollywood A-Lister, with

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:56.860
The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air having ended its
run just weeks before Independence Day hit

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:57.720
theaters.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:01.040
"I make this look good."

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:06.069
Smith wasn't the first choice to play rookie
Agent J in Men in Black, however.

00:10:06.069 --> 00:10:10.949
Another actor known primarily for one huge
TV role was offered it first: David Schwimmer,

00:10:10.949 --> 00:10:15.330
known to millions as lovelorn paleontologist
Ross on Friends.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:19.449
While he was shooting the comedy The Pallbearer
for Miramax, the studio was so confident the

00:10:19.449 --> 00:10:23.940
future box office bomb would be a hit that
it wanted to sign Schwimmer to a three-movie

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:24.940
deal.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:28.820
Schwimmer agreed to it if they let him also
direct Since You've Been Gone, and cast everyone

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:31.209
from his Chicago theater company.

00:10:31.209 --> 00:10:34.490
The film went into production, and Schwimmer
gave all of his friends roles.

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:36.639
He explained on the Awards Chatter podcast,

00:10:36.639 --> 00:10:40.500
"About a month before production, I get the
call about Men in Black.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.980
Which was a direct conflict with directing
that film.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:44.420
I just said, 'I can't.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:48.380
These are my closest friends in the world,
this is their first shot at a movie, my first

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:49.500
shot at directing."

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:53.690
Nor could he delay filming the movie in favor
of Men in Black, because he'd be due back

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:54.839
at Friends.

00:10:54.839 --> 00:10:58.410
MiB producers had to move on, and they picked
Smith.

